# notes : too add service as system level you need to add the sevice_name.service to /etc/systemd/system/ which  is owned by the root so privilege permissions are required this why it came the option of creatinig   the service as user by create  the file here :
      > nano ~/.config/systemd/user/miniserve.service
              or use this command 
      > systemctl  edit --force --full --user miniserver
      
 paste :
[Unit]
Description=Miniserve (User Service)
After=network.target

[Service]
WorkingDirectory=/home/yasine/Downloads
ExecStart=/usr/bin/miniserve .
Restart=always
RestartSec=3

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target  # to link a service to system  boot process 

# and to check use :
  > systemctl cat miniserver --user    # it used above to see the service file of service
  

# Reload user daemon :
 > systemctl --user daemon-reload
# Step 4: Start it:
 > systemctl --user start miniserve
 
 
# Check:

  > systemctl --user status miniserve

# for all steps check the videos inside folder systemd_service 

# Make it run even after logout (important)

By default, user services stop when you log out.

To fix that:
  > loginctl show-user yasine   # very hopeful loginctl command (loging manager) it info about the user 
  > sudo loginctl enable-linger yasine  # this enable the ( var : linger)  

        👉 This allows your user’s systemd to run without an active login session

# Logs (user version)
journalctl --user -u miniserve -f

# remember : 
   system's  services are stored in : /etc/systemd/system/   or in  /usr/lib/systemd/system/
   user's services are stored in : ~/.config/systemd/user/
  
  
